  "answer": "Mendel’s monohybrid experiment involved crossing pea plants with different traits. He started with two purebred pea plants: one with yellow seeds and one with green seeds. When he crossed these plants, all the offspring in the first generation (F1) had yellow seeds. This showed that the yellow seed trait was dominant. When these F1 plants were allowed to self-pollinate, the second generation (F2) showed a 3:1 ratio of yellow to green seeds. This demonstrated the law of dominance, where the dominant trait (yellow) masks the recessive trait (green) in the F1 generation but reappears in the F2 generation in a predictable ratio.",
